  <li><a id="n3"></a>See, for example,  Claudio Borio, <a href="https://www.bis.org/speeches/sp230426.htm" target="_blank">&ldquo;Getting up from the floor,&rdquo;</a> remarks at the Netherlands Bank Workshop  &ldquo;Beyond unconventional policy: Implications for central banks&rsquo; operational  frameworks,&rdquo; March 10, 2023.</li>
  <li><a id="n4"></a>Lorie K. Logan, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2020/log201201" target="_blank">&ldquo;A Return to Operating with  Abundant Reserves,&rdquo;</a> remarks before the Money Marketeers of New York  University, Dec. 1, 2020.</li>
  <li><a id="n5"></a>Lorie K. Logan, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2019/log190417" target="_blank">&ldquo;Observations on Implementing  Monetary Policy in an Ample-Reserves Regime,&rdquo;</a> remarks before the Money Marketeers of New York  University, April 17, 2019.</li>
  <li><a id="n6"></a>For a formal model,  see Gara Afonso, Kyungmin Kim, Antoine Martin, Ed Nosal, Simon Potter and Sam  Schulhofer-Wohl, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/research/staff_reports/sr910.pdf" target="_blank">&ldquo;Monetary Policy Implementation  with Ample Reserves,&rdquo;</a> Federal Reserve Bank of New York Staff Report No.  910, revised July 2023.</li>
  <li><a id="n7"></a>Lorie K. Logan, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2020/log200715" target="_blank">&ldquo;The Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Market  Functioning Purchases: From Supporting to Sustaining,&rdquo;</a> remarks at SIFMA Webinar, July 15, 2020.</li>
  <li><a id="n8"></a>Lorie K. Logan, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2021/log210415" target="_blank">&ldquo;Impact of Abundant Reserves on  Money Markets and Policy Implementation,&rdquo;</a> remarks at SIFMA Webinar, April 15, 2021.</li>
  <li><a id="n9"></a>See <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/markets/omo/omo2008.pdf" target="_blank">&ldquo;Domestic Open Market Operations  During 2008,&rdquo;</a> report prepared for  the Federal Open Market Committee by the Markets Group of the Federal Reserve  Bank of New York, January 2009. </li>
  <li><a id="n10"></a>Sam Schulhofer-Wohl, <a href="https://www.chicagofed.org/~/media/publications/chicago-fed-letter/2019/cfl423-pdf.pdf" target="_blank">&ldquo;Understanding recent fluctuations  in short-term interest rates,&rdquo;</a> <em>Chicago Fed  Letter</em> No. 423, 2019.</li>
  <li><a id="n11"></a>See, for example,  Adam Copeland, Linsey Molloy, and Anya Tarascina, <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2019/02/what-can-we-learn-from-the-timing-of-interbank-payments/" target="_blank">&ldquo;What Can We Learn from the Timing  of Interbank Payments?&rdquo;</a>, Liberty Street Economics, Feb. 25, 2019. </li>
  <li><a id="n12"></a>For more on  principles that should guide central bank interventions to address market  dysfunction, see Lorie K. Logan, <a href="/news/speeches/logan/2023/lkl230303">&ldquo;Preventing and responding to  dysfunction in core markets,&rdquo;</a> remarks at the Workshop on Market Dysfunction at the  University of Chicago Booth School of Business, March 3, 2023.</li>
  <li><a id="n13"></a>See James Clouse, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/1194lead.pdf" target="_blank">&ldquo;Recent Developments in Discount  Window Policy,&rdquo;</a> Federal Reserve Bulletin, Nov. 1994, 965-977;  Brian Madigan and William Nelson, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/pubs/bulletin/2002/0702lead.pdf" target="_blank">&ldquo;Proposed Revision to the Federal  Reserve's Discount Window Lending Programs,&rdquo;</a> Federal Reserve Bulletin, July 2002, 313-319;  Olivier Armantier, Helene Lee, and Asani Sarkar, <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2015/08/history-of-discount-window-stigma/" target="_blank">&ldquo;History of Discount Window  Stigma,&rdquo;</a> Liberty Street  Economics, Aug. 10, 2015; and Mark Carlson and Jonathan D. Rose, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/stigma-and-the-discount-window-20171219.html" target="_blank">&ldquo;Stigma and the discount window,&rdquo;</a> FEDS Notes, Dec. 19, 2017.</li>
  <li><a id="n14"></a>Federal Open Market  Committee, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20210728b.htm" target="_blank">&ldquo;Statement Regarding Repurchase  Agreement Arrangements,&rdquo;</a> July 28, 2021.</li>
  <li><a id="n15"></a>See Board of  Governors of the Federal Reserve System, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/fomcminutes20210616.pdf" target="_blank">&ldquo;Minutes of the Federal Open Market  Committee, June 15-16, 2021,&rdquo;</a> 2021.</li>
  <li><a id="n16"></a>See Andrew Hauser, <a href="https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/speech/2023/september/andrew-hauser-speech-at-market-news-international-connect-event" target="_blank">&ldquo;A journey of 1000 miles begins  with a single step: filling gaps in the central bank liquidity toolkit,&rdquo;</a> speech at a Market News International Connect  Event, Charter Accountants&rsquo; Hall, London, Sept. 28, 2023.</li>
  <li><a id="n17"></a>For additional  discussion of interbank trading in different implementation regimes, see Simon  Potter, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2016/pot160826" target="_blank">&ldquo;Discussion of &lsquo;Evaluating Monetary  Policy Operational Frameworks&rsquo; by Ulrich Bindseil,&rdquo;</a> remarks at the Jackson Hole Economic Policy  Symposium, Aug. 26, 2016.</li>
  <li><a id="n18"></a>See Lorie K. Logan, <a href="https://www.dallasfed.org/news/speeches/logan/2023/lkl230518">&ldquo;Remarks on liquidity provision and  on the economic outlook and monetary policy,&rdquo;</a> remarks to the Texas Bankers Association, San  Antonio, May 18, 2023.</li>
  <li><a id="n19"></a>For a discussion of  frictions in collateral movement during the March 2023 banking stresses, see  Richard Ostrander, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2023/ost230617" target="_blank">&ldquo;Remarks on the Panel &lsquo;Bank Crisis  Framework: Learning from Experience,&rsquo;&rdquo;</a> Paris Meeting of the Committee on International  Monetary Law of the International Law Association, June 17, 2023.</li>
  <li><a id="n20"></a>In the U.S., the  Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation Improvement Act of 1991 limits discount  window lending to undercapitalized depository institutions.</li>
  <li><a id="n21"></a>See Kyungmin Kim,  Antoine Martin and Ed Nosal, <a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/jmcb.12693" target="_blank">&ldquo;Can the interbank market be revived?&rdquo;</a>, <em>Journal  of Money, Credit and Banking </em>52(7), 1645-1689, October 2020.</li>
  <li><a id="n22"></a>See Randal K.  Quarles, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/quarles20200206a.htm" target="_blank">&ldquo;The Economic Outlook, Monetary  Policy, and the Demand for Reserves,&rdquo;</a> speech at the Money Marketeers of New York University,  Feb. 6, 2020.</li>
  <li><a id="n23"></a>Lorie K. Logan, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2020/log201023" target="_blank">&ldquo;Treasury Market Liquidity and  Early Lessons from the Pandemic Shock,&rdquo;</a> remarks at Brookings-Chicago Booth Task Force  on Financial Stability meeting, Oct. 23, 2020.</li>
  <li><a id="n24"></a>A central bank&rsquo;s  charter and the financial system in which it operates may also influence the  considerations surrounding the optimal supply of reserves. For a discussion of  these and other factors in the U.K. context, see Andrew Hauser, <a href="https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/speech/2023/november/andrew-hauser-keynote-speech-bank-of-england-watchers-conference" target="_blank">&ldquo;&lsquo;Less is more or&rsquo; or &lsquo;Less is a  bore&rsquo;? Re-calibrating the role of central bank reserves,&rdquo;</a> speech at Kings College London&rsquo;s Bank of  England Watchers&rsquo; Conference, Nov. 3, 2023.</li>
  <li><a id="n25"></a>See, for example,  Darrell Duffie and Arvind Krishnamurthy, <a href="https://www.kansascityfed.org/Jackson%20Hole/documents/7030/Duffie_JH2016.pdf" target="_blank">&ldquo;Pass-Through Efficiency in the  Fed&rsquo;s New Monetary Policy Setting,&rdquo;</a> paper presented at the Jackson Hole Economic Policy  Symposium, Aug. 26, 2016, and Simon Potter, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2017/pot170405" target="_blank">&ldquo;Money Markets at a Crossroads:  Policy Implementation at a Time of Structural Change,&rdquo;</a> remarks at the Master of Applied Economics&rsquo;  Distinguished Speaker Series, University of California, Los Angeles, April 5,  2017.</li>
  <li><a id="n26"></a>See Annette  Vissing-Jørgensen, <a href="https://www.ecb.europa.eu/pub/conferences/ecbforum/shared/pdf/2023/VissingJorgensen_paper.pdf" target="_blank">&ldquo;Balance Sheet Policy Above the  ELB,&rdquo;</a> paper presented at  the ECB Forum on Central Banking, June 28, 2023.</li>
</ol>
                        
                        
